@* Who is on this one server, who is allowed to be, and what they may do — one
   table over three sources, because they describe the same people. The allowlist
   and the operator overrides live here and only here, because Bedrock keeps both
   files inside each server's own folder (docs/design.md § 7.5, D31). *@

@using BedrockSentry.Core.Players
@using BedrockSentry.Host.Components.Players
@using BedrockSentry.Services.Instances

@implements IAsyncDisposable

@inject ServerAccessControl Access
@inject IPlayerStore Players
@inject TimeProvider Time

<h3>Players on this server</h3>

<p class="lead">
    Who may join this server and what they can do. Playtime is this server's own,
    not the all-time totals on the <a href="players">Players</a> page.
</p>

@if (fileError is not null)
{
    <p class="console-fault">
        Could not read this server's access files — @fileError
    </p>
}

@* T231. Three arms, not two, and the bar does not render until the file has been
   read at all. An unreadable server.properties used to fall through to the else
   below and assert "Anyone can join" -- a claim about ACCESS CONTROL made from a
   file the wrapper could not open. The Status tab one click away was already
   honest about the same file, which is the tell this codebase keeps filing: two
   surfaces, one fact, one of them guessing.

   The bar is inside the `loaded` gate, and the reason first written here was an
   OVER-CLAIM: it said every page load showed "Anyone can join" until the read
   returned. On this path it never did. `ReloadAsync` has one await, `ListAsync`,
   and Microsoft.Data.Sqlite overrides none of the async members, so the component
   renders once with `loaded` already true and the unloaded frame does not exist.
   Sampled on two instances and seen in zero renders.

   The gate stays, and on the honest reason rather than the observed one: a bar
   outside it would be a claim about access control that nothing had established,
   and what makes that frame unreachable today is a detail of one provider's async
   implementation rather than anything this file controls. A claim nobody can
   currently see is still a claim nothing supports.

   Worth keeping as the shape, since this comment is inside a fix for the class
   defined by comments that state what the code does not do: an over-claim in a
   `.razor` file cannot be failed by the suite, so it survives exactly as long as
   nobody re-reads it. *@
@if (loaded)
{
<div class="access-bar">
    @if (!propertiesKnown)
    {
        <span class="state-badge state-warning">Allowlist unknown</span>
        <span class="counts">
            This server's <code>server.properties</code> could not be read, so
            whether the allow list is enforced is unknown.
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(propertiesWhy))
            {
                <text>Windows said: @propertiesWhy</text>
            }
        </span>
    }
    else if (allowlistEnforced)
    {
        <span class="state-badge state-running">Allowlist enabled</span>
        <span class="counts">
            <b>@rows.Count(r => r.Allowed)</b> of <b>@rows.Count</b> known
            @(rows.Count == 1 ? "player" : "players") may join
        </span>
    }
    else
    {
        <span class="state-badge state-info">Allowlist disabled</span>
        <span class="counts">
            Anyone can join. Entries are kept and apply when <code>allow-list</code>
            is turned on in this server's
            <a href="@($"instances/{Supervisor.InstanceId}/properties")">properties</a>.
        </span>
    }
</div>
}

@if (!loaded)
{
    <p class="empty-state">Reading this server's players…</p>
}
else if (rows.Count == 0)
{
    <p class="empty-state">
        Nobody has connected to this server and nothing is on its allowlist yet.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th aria-sort="@AriaSort("player")">
                        <button type="button" class="sort-head" @onclick='() => SortBy("player")'>
                            Player @Caret("player")
                        </button>
                    </th>
                    <th>Allow list</th>
                    <th>Join if full</th>
                    <th>Role</th>
                    <th class="num" aria-sort="@AriaSort("playtime")">
                        <button type="button" class="sort-head" @onclick='() => SortBy("playtime")'>
                            Playtime @Caret("playtime")
                        </button>
                    </th>
                    <th class="num" aria-sort="@AriaSort("last")">
                        <button type="button" class="sort-head" @onclick='() => SortBy("last")'>
                            Last seen @Caret("last")
                        </button>
                    </th>
                    @if (DeathsSince is not null)
                    {
                        <th class="num" aria-sort="@AriaSort("deaths")">
                            <button type="button" class="sort-head" @onclick='() => SortBy("deaths")'>
                                Deaths @Caret("deaths")
                            </button>
                        </th>
                    }
                    <th class="actions"></th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Visible())
                {
                    <tr @key="row.Xuid ?? row.Gamertag" class="@(row.IsOnline ? "roster-online" : null)">
                        <td>
                            @if (row.Xuid is { Length: > 0 } xuid)
                            {
                                <div class="player-cell">
                                    <PlayerFaceImage Xuid="@xuid" />
                                    <div>
                                        <a href="@($"players/{xuid}")"><strong>@row.Display</strong></a>
                                        <span class="roster-gamertag">@xuid</span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <strong>@row.Display</strong>
                                @* Bedrock fills the XUID in on a first join, and
                                   permissions.json is keyed on it, so this row's
                                   role cannot be set until then (D31). *@
                                <span class="access-note">never joined — no XUID yet</span>
                            }
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <input type="checkbox" checked="@row.Allowed"
                                   disabled="@(busy || !row.AllowIsSettable)"
                                   aria-label="@($"{row.Display} is on the allow list")"
                                   @onchange="e => SetAllowedAsync(row, (bool?)e.Value ?? false)" />
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            @* No console command sets ignoresPlayerLimit, so it is a
                               stopped-server edit. Disabled rather than hidden, so the
                               value is still readable while the server runs. *@
                            <input type="checkbox" checked="@row.IgnoresPlayerLimit"
                                   disabled="@(busy || !row.Allowed || !row.AllowIsSettable || !ServerIsStopped)"
                                   aria-label="@($"{row.Display} may join a full server")"
                                   title="@(ServerIsStopped
                                       ? null
                                       : "Bedrock has no console command for this — stop the server to change it")"
                                   @onchange="e => SetIgnoresLimitAsync(row, (bool?)e.Value ?? false)" />
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <select class="form-select form-select-sm role"
                                    disabled="@(busy || !row.RoleIsSettable)"
                                    value="@row.Tier"
                                    @onchange="e => SetTierAsync(row, e.Value?.ToString())">
                                <option value="@PermissionTier.Visitor">visitor</option>
                                <option value="@PermissionTier.Member">member</option>
                                <option value="@PermissionTier.Operator">operator</option>
                            </select>
                        </td>

                        <td class="num">@PlayerFormat.Duration(row.Playtime)</td>

                        <td class="num">
                            @if (row.Session is { } session)
                            {
                                @* The live session length rather than the word "now":
                                   same cell, same width, and "on for four hours" is
                                   the fact somebody is actually looking for. *@
                                <span class="state-badge state-running">@PlayerFormat.Duration(session)</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <span class="text-nowrap">@PlayerFormat.DateAndTime(row.LastSeenUtc)</span>

                                @* T293: beside the last-seen time it belongs to, where that
                                   session ended. *@
                                @if (row.LastKnownPosition is { } where)
                                {
                                    <div class="check-detail">@DimensionNames.Describe(where)</div>
                                }
                            }
                        </td>

                        @if (DeathsSince is not null)
                        {
                            <td class="num">@DeathText.Count(row.Deaths)</td>
                        }

                        <td>
                            @if (row.IsOnline && !ServerIsStopped)
                            {
                                <div class="row-actions">
                                    <button class="btn btn-sm btn-secondary" disabled="@busy"
                                            @onclick="() => KickAsync(row)">
                                        Kick
                                    </button>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    @if (DeathsSince is { } since)
    {
        <p class="check-detail">@DeathText.Coverage(PlayerFormat.Date(since))</p>
    }
}

<div class="provision-controls">
    <input class="form-control" placeholder="Gamertag, exactly as Xbox spells it"
           style="max-width: 20rem"
           value="@newName" @oninput="e => newName = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty"
           disabled="@busy" />
    <button class="btn btn-primary" disabled="@(busy || newName.Trim().Length == 0)"
            @onclick="AllowAsync">
        Add to allow list
    </button>
    @if (!ServerIsStopped)
    {
        <span class="check-detail">
            Server running — changes go through its console.
        </span>
    }
</div>

@if (message is not null)
{
    <p class="@(messageIsFault ? "console-fault" : "check-detail")">@message</p>
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public InstanceSupervisor Supervisor { get; set; } = default!;

    private IReadOnlyList<InstanceAccessRow> rows = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<AllowlistEntry> allowlist = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<PermissionEntry> permissions = [];
    // Off until the file says otherwise, matching BDS's own default. This
    // started as `true`, which meant an unreadable or not-yet-loaded file
    // claimed the allowlist was being enforced (`T136`).
    private bool allowlistEnforced;

    // T231. No forgiving default, because nothing renders this until `loaded` is
    // true and the read has set it. A default of `true` here would be the same
    // false clear one frame wide -- the page claiming to know before it had looked.
    private bool propertiesKnown;

    private string? propertiesWhy;
    private string defaultTier = PermissionTier.Member;
    private string? fileError;

    private string newName = string.Empty;
    private bool busy;
    private bool loaded;
    private string? message;
    private bool messageIsFault;

    private PeriodicTimer? timer;
    private CancellationTokenSource? cts;
    private DateTimeOffset now;

    /// Newest activity first, and online above offline whichever way it sorts —
    /// an offline row has no session to compare.
    private string sortKey = "last";
    private bool descending = true;

    /// The instance the table was filled for. The hosting page re-renders every
    /// second; refilling on the tick is the bug T22 was.
    private string? seededFor;

    private bool ServerIsStopped =>
        Supervisor.Status.State is Core.Instances.InstanceState.Stopped
            or Core.Instances.InstanceState.Faulted
            or Core.Instances.InstanceState.Backoff;

    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        if (seededFor != Supervisor.InstanceId)
        {
            seededFor = Supervisor.InstanceId;
            newName = string.Empty;
            message = null;
            loaded = false;

            await ReloadAsync();
        }
    }

    protected override void OnAfterRender(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        cts = new CancellationTokenSource();

        // Every three seconds, matching the roster this replaced: sessions change
        // on the timescale of somebody walking away from a keyboard, and both
        // access files are cheap to stat.
        timer = new PeriodicTimer(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(3));
        _ = PollAsync(cts.Token);
    }

    private async Task PollAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        try
        {
            while (await timer!.WaitForNextTickAsync(cancellationToken))
            {
                if (!busy)
                {
                    await ReloadAsync();
                    await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
                }
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Navigating away, or the circuit went.
        }
    }

    private string AllowlistPath => AllowlistFile.PathFor(Supervisor.WorkingDirectory);
    private string PermissionsPath => PermissionsFile.PathFor(Supervisor.WorkingDirectory);

    private async Task ReloadAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            allowlist = AllowlistFile.Load(AllowlistPath).Entries;
            permissions = PermissionsFile.Load(PermissionsPath).Entries;

            var read = ServerProperties.ReadFile(
                ServerProperties.PathFor(Supervisor.WorkingDirectory));

            var properties = read.Values;
            propertiesKnown = read.Known;
            propertiesWhy = read.Why;

            allowlistEnforced = ServerProperties.AllowlistEnforced(properties);

            defaultTier = InstanceAccessView.DefaultTier(properties);

            now = Time.GetUtcNow();

            rows = InstanceAccessView.Build(
                await Players.ListAsync(Supervisor.InstanceId, now),
                allowlist,
                permissions,
                defaultTier,
                now);

            fileError = null;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Said rather than swallowed: an empty table and a broken database
            // look identical, and only one of them needs somebody to act.
            fileError = ex.Message;
        }
        finally
        {
            loaded = true;
        }
    }

    /// When the companion pack first reported on this server, which every row's
    /// tally shares; null when it never has, and then there is no Deaths column.
    private DateTimeOffset? DeathsSince => DeathTally.CountedSince(rows.Select(r => r.Deaths));

    private IEnumerable<InstanceAccessRow> Visible() => sortKey switch
    {
        "player" => Order(rows, r => r.Display),
        "playtime" => Order(rows, r => r.Playtime),
        "deaths" => Order(rows, r => r.Deaths?.Count ?? -1),

        _ => Order(rows, r => r.LastSeenUtc ?? DateTimeOffset.MinValue),
    };

    /// Online always above offline, whichever key and whichever direction — an
    /// offline row has no session, and floating those through the middle of the
    /// column would be sorting on a value that does not exist.
    ///
    /// The pin lives here rather than in each branch (`T132`). It used to be
    /// written out in the last-seen branch only, so every other key sorted on the
    /// key alone and filed the online rows alphabetically among people who were
    /// not there — with a comment above the switch promising the opposite. One
    /// place to be right is the fix; three places to remember is what broke it.
    private IOrderedEnumerable<InstanceAccessRow> Order<TKey>(
        IEnumerable<InstanceAccessRow> source, Func<InstanceAccessRow, TKey> key) =>
        descending
            ? source.OrderByDescending(r => r.IsOnline).ThenByDescending(key)
            : source.OrderByDescending(r => r.IsOnline).ThenBy(key);

    private void SortBy(string key)
    {
        if (sortKey == key)
        {
            descending = !descending;
            return;
        }

        sortKey = key;

        // Names read naturally A–Z; the other two are columns where the
        // interesting end is the big one.
        descending = key != "player";
    }

    private string? Caret(string key) => sortKey == key ? (descending ? "▾" : "▴") : null;

    private string AriaSort(string key) =>
        sortKey != key ? "none" : descending ? "descending" : "ascending";

    private async Task SetAllowedAsync(InstanceAccessRow row, bool allowed)
    {
        await RunAsync(async () =>
        {
            // Unticking is the removal — there is no separate Remove button,
            // because a button beside a checkbox that does the same thing is
            // the duplication the merge exists to get rid of.
            var change = allowed
                ? await Access.AllowAsync(Supervisor, row.Gamertag)
                : await Access.DisallowAsync(Supervisor, row.Gamertag);

            Report(change);
        });
    }

    private async Task SetIgnoresLimitAsync(InstanceAccessRow row, bool value)
    {
        // Re-checked here and not only in the markup. A running BDS owns its own
        // file and rewrites it on any in-game change (D31), so an edit fired from
        // a render that went stale would be lost at best and would land on top of
        // the server's own write at worst.
        if (!ServerIsStopped)
        {
            return;
        }

        await RunAsync(() =>
        {
            var file = AllowlistFile.Load(AllowlistPath);
            file.SetIgnoresPlayerLimit(row.Gamertag, value);
            file.Save(AllowlistPath);

            message = value
                ? $"{row.Display} may now join a full server."
                : $"{row.Display} no longer ignores the player limit.";
            messageIsFault = false;

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        });
    }

    private async Task SetTierAsync(InstanceAccessRow row, string? tier)
    {
        if (row.Xuid is not { Length: > 0 } xuid || tier is null || tier == row.Tier)
        {
            return;
        }

        await RunAsync(async () =>
        {
            if (tier == PermissionTier.Visitor)
            {
                // op/deop only reach two of the three tiers, so visitor is always
                // a file edit — which a running server would overwrite.
                if (!ServerIsStopped)
                {
                    message =
                        $"Bedrock has no console command for visitor, so {row.Display} can "
                        + "only be set to it while the server is stopped.";
                    messageIsFault = true;
                    return;
                }

                var file = PermissionsFile.Load(PermissionsPath);
                file.Set(xuid, PermissionTier.Visitor);
                file.Save(PermissionsPath);

                message = $"{row.Display} is now visitor.";
                messageIsFault = false;
                return;
            }

            Report(await Access.SetOperatorAsync(
                Supervisor, xuid, row.Gamertag, tier == PermissionTier.Operator));
        });
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Not routed through <see cref="ServerAccessControl"/>: a kick edits neither
    /// access file, so there is nothing for that class's read-the-file
    /// confirmation to check. The confirmation is the row going offline on the
    /// next poll, which is a fact the page already has.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task KickAsync(InstanceAccessRow row)
    {
        await RunAsync(async () =>
        {
            await Supervisor.SendCommandAsync($"kick \"{row.Gamertag}\"");

            message = $"Kicked {row.Display}.";
            messageIsFault = false;
        });
    }

    private async Task AllowAsync()
    {
        await RunAsync(async () =>
        {
            var change = await Access.AllowAsync(Supervisor, newName);
            Report(change);

            if (change.Changed)
            {
                newName = string.Empty;
            }
        });
    }

    private void Report(AccessChange change)
    {
        message = change.Detail;
        messageIsFault = !change.ConfirmedOnDisk;
    }

    private async Task RunAsync(Func<Task> action)
    {
        if (busy)
        {
            return;
        }

        busy = true;

        try
        {
            await action();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            message = ex.Message;
            messageIsFault = true;
        }
        finally
        {
            busy = false;
            await ReloadAsync();
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        timer?.Dispose();

        if (cts is not null)
        {
            await cts.CancelAsync();
            cts.Dispose();
        }
    }
}
